<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/water-resources-and-pollution">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Water pollution is <strong>chemistry plus plumbing plus governance</strong>: the science is
    mature (we know what each contaminant does and how to remove it), which makes the remaining
    problems — runoff, legacy contamination, underinvestment — engineering and political
    problems, not scientific ones.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the taxonomy, eutrophication, groundwater, treatment" widget="equationStep" config={waterPollCfg} />

  <h2>Cases that shaped policy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Cuyahoga River fire (1969):</strong> an oil-slicked river burning helped create the US EPA and Clean Water Act (1972) — the river now hosts fish. Point-source regulation is environmentalism\'s clearest success story.</li>
    <li><strong>Flint (2014–):</strong> switching water sources without corrosion control leached lead from pipes — a chemistry failure with an accountability failure inside it. The lesson: treatment chemistry is public health.</li>
    <li><strong>PFAS everywhere:</strong> "forever chemicals" (non-stick, waterproofing) now appear in blood of nearly all humans — persistent, mobile, health-linked at low doses. Regulation is catching up (EPA limits 2024); remediation is genuinely hard.</li>
    <li><strong>Wastewater reuse is mainstreaming:</strong> Singapore\'s NEWater and California\'s direct potable reuse close the loop — drinking-water standards applied to recycled water; technology ahead of public comfort.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> US EPA Clean Water Act history; WHO drinking-water guidelines; NOAA Gulf dead zone monitoring; the water-cycle lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
